<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Auth's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because almost every
    consumer is in Base: IRoleAssignmentService alone is injected by ~30 production files —
    Humans.Application services (Users, Profiles, Shifts, GoogleIntegration), Humans.Infrastructure
    jobs and the metrics service, Shell's claims transformation and admin controllers, and twelve
    already-moved sections. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Auth would therefore make Base
    reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    RoleAssignmentOperationRequirement — an IAuthorizationRequirement Shell's
    UsersAdminController passes to IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync — sits in Humans.Auth's
    own Contracts/ *folder* rather than here. Tickets was the first section to ship both halves;
    the split is what each may name (template step 5b).

    That placement stands, but its old stated reason does not. This comment used to read
    "framework-free by construction (Microsoft.NET.Sdk; no FrameworkReference)", i.e. that the
    leaf *could not* name an ASP.NET type. Measured false, G5 lane 3c, 2026-08-15:
    Humans.Interfaces has carried FrameworkReference Microsoft.AspNetCore.App since ISection
    landed, and FrameworkReference flows transitively through ProjectReference, so this leaf
    resolves Microsoft.AspNetCore.App with IsTransitiveFrameworkReference=true. It could name
    IAuthorizationRequirement today and the build would not complain.

    The reason to keep the requirement out is therefore a choice, not a constraint: Base
    consumers of this leaf want Auth's *vocabulary*, not its ASP.NET authorization plumbing, and
    the one type that is plumbing has exactly one caller, in Shell. The rule that is actually
    mechanical here is the ProjectReference set below — see Humans.Interfaces.csproj for the
    single invariant (zero Humans.* ProjectReferences on Base; external packages unrestricted,
    Peter 2026-08-15). Enforcement of "this leaf names no ASP.NET type" now lives entirely in
    AuthArchitectureTests.ContractsLeafNamesNoAspNetType, which inspects the emitted assembly's
    referenced-assembly list. Delete that test and nothing else is watching.

    AssignRoleAsync/EndRoleAsync return the section's own RoleAssignmentResult rather than
    Humans.Onboarding.Contracts.OnboardingResult, which they returned in Base.

    Humans.Users.Contracts is the one leaf-to-leaf reference here, added with IMagicLinkService
    at nobodies-collective/Humans#866 G5 lane 4b-2i. It is forced, not chosen: two of that
    interface's members return Humans.Users.Contracts.User, and its Base consumer
    (Humans.Application's ExternalLoginService, which calls FindUserByVerifiedEmailAsync) cannot
    reference the Humans.Auth *section* project — design §15 step 5b's "a consumer in Base forces
    Humans.<Section>.Contracts", the same shape that put IUserService on the Users leaf.

    It is NOT an unwind item, and the 2026-08-14 design's "each gets unwound" phrasing was
    corrected in G5 lane 3c. Humans.Users.Contracts holds exactly one ProjectReference of its own
    (Humans.Onboarding.Contracts, which holds none), so this chain terminates and cannot cycle.
    The rule is: a leaf may reference Base and any leaf that itself has zero project references.
    Of the two edges that design bullet named for unwinding, Teams.Contracts → Auth.Contracts
    turned out to be a dead line (deleted in 3c) and Email.Contracts → Events.Contracts
    terminates in a zero-reference leaf. Neither was ever an unwind.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Users.Contracts\Humans.Users.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
